oca, “On the list version of a conjecture of Erdős and Neumann-Lara”, arXiv:2603.01020 (2026).\n\n# List Erdős–Neumann-Lara conjecture\n\nLess than 1 year old · traced to 2026\n\nLet $G$G be a graph. Its list chromatic number $\\chi_{\\ell}(G)$χ ℓ​(G) is the least integer $r$r such that every assignment of lists of $r$r colours to the vertices admits a proper list colouring. For an orientation $D$D of $G$G, define its list dichromatic number $\\vec\\chi_{\\ell}(D)$χ​ℓ​(D) analogously, using list colourings in which every colour class induces an acyclic digraph, and let\n\n$$\\vec\\chi_{\\ell}(G)=\\max_D\\vec\\chi_{\\ell}(D),$$χ​ℓ​(G)=D max​χ​ℓ​(D),\n\nwhere the maximum is over all orientations $D$D of $G$G.\n\nList Erdős–Neumann-Lara conjecture. For every integer $k$k there is an integer $f(k)$f(k) such that, for every graph $G$G, $\\chi_{\\ell}(G)\\geq f(k)$χ ℓ​(G)≥f(k) implies $\\vec\\chi_{\\ell}(G)\\geq k$χ​ℓ​(G)≥k.\n\nThe paper states that it proves this list version of the Erdős–Neumann-Lara conjecture, so the claim is resolved by the source's results.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Ararat Harutyunyan, Lucas Picasarri-Arrieta and Gil Puig i Surroca, “On the list version of a conjecture of Erdős and Neumann-Lara”, arXiv:2603.01020 (2026).\n\ndichromatic-numberdigraphslist-coloring\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Refreshed 1 month ago\n\nOpen · solution reported\n\nA 2026 preprint claims to prove the conjecture, but the proof has not been independently verified.\n\nThe conjecture asks whether sufficiently large list chromatic number forces arbitrarily large list dichromatic number. The retrieved preprint presents a proof of this implication.\n\n2026 preprint claiming a proof\n\nThe paper claims that every graph of minimum degree $d$d satisfies $\\vec{\\chi}_{\\ell}(G)\\geq (\\frac{1}{3}-o(1))\\log_{2}d$χ​ℓ​(G)≥(3 1​−o(1))lo g 2​d, and derives $\\vec{\\chi}_{\\ell}(G)\\geq (\\frac{1}{3}-o(1))\\log_{2}\\chi_{\\ell}(G)$χ​ℓ​(G)≥(3 1​−o(1))lo g 2​χ ℓ​(G). It explicitly identifies this as a proof of the list Erdős–Neumann–Lara conjecture. A stated quantitative consequence is that, for sufficiently large $r$r, average degree at least $r^{7}2^{3r+17}$r 7 2 3 r+17 implies $\\vec{\\chi}_{\\ell}(G)\\geq r$χ​ℓ​(G)≥r. No independent verification, error report, withdrawal, or competing proof was found.\n\nCurrent status (as of September 2026): The conjecture is claimed solved by the 2026 preprint, but its proof remains unverified in the retrieved record.\n\nSources\n\n  * arxiv.org ↗--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Complexity of Chess Domination Problems (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/365299316_Complexity_of_Chess_Domination_Problems)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; The minimal independent chessboard domination queen completion problem is NP-complete.\nAnalogously, we deﬁne the minimal independent chessboard domination queen completion prob-\n\nlem (n,Q, l)I. Given a set Q of k non-attacking queens placed on the n×n chessboard, is there a set Q′\n\nof size k+l such that Q⊂Q′, that it guards the chessboard, and no pair of queens in Q′attack each\n\nother. Notice that the di ﬀ erence with the well-known n-queens completion problem is that with n\n\nqueens the n×n chessboard is trivially dominated. As it has already been proven that this problem\n\nis NP-complete [9, Thm 37], we can prove the following corollary.\n\nCorollary 24. The minimal independent chessboard domination queen completion problem is NP-complete.\n\nProof. Let n∈N and 0 ≤k≤n. An instance, (n, Q) of the n–queens completion problem can be\n\nreduced in polynomial time to an instance of the minimal independent chessboard domination queen\n\ncompletion problem (n,Q, n −k)I. Since n–queens completion is NP-complete [9, Thm 37], it is NP-\n\nhard and minimal independent domination queen completion problem is NP-hard. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n22102026 | Faculty of Mathematics and Physics (https://www.mff.cuni.cz/en/kam/teaching-and-seminars/noon-lectures/2026/22102026)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; October 22, 2026, 12:20 in S6 ... Given any finite set of points in the plane, Neumann-Lara and Urrutia proved in 1988 that there is always a pair such that every circle through the two points encloses a positive fraction of all points. ... A related conjecture by Ramos and Viaña on the depth of pairs in point sets in convex position in 3-space falls as well.\n# The deepest pairs of points are not as deep as conjectured\n\n## Emo Welzl\n\n### ETH Zurich\n\nOctober 22, 2026, 12:20 in S6\n\n## Abstract\n\nGiven any finite set of points in the plane, Neumann-Lara and Urrutia proved in 1988 that there is always a pair such that every circle through the two points encloses a positive fraction of all points. In 1989, Edelsbrunner, Hasan, Seidel and Shen showed that a fraction of 1/2 − 1/√12 = 0.211... can be guaranteed, while Hayward, Rappaport and Wenger showed that more than 1/4 = 0.25 cannot. In 2002, Urrutia conjectured that 1/4 is best possible. We disprove this by showing that 3/14 = 0.214... cannot be exceeded. A related conjecture by Ramos and Viaña on the depth of pairs in point sets in convex position in 3-space falls as well.\n\nIt all boils down to realizing special pseudomanifolds, namely twofold triple systems, as the collections of j-facets of point sets in 3-space, and to blowing up such point sets. We designed the necessary properties of the point sets, while AI assistants were instrumental in realizing them.\n\nJoint work with H. Edelsbrunner, A. Garber, and M. Saghafian.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2607.13895] Feedback vertex sets in oriented graphs1 - Academus scientific article reader (https://academ.us/article/2607.13895/)\nciteturn6search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: last month; If true, the conjecture would be tight since Knauer, Valicov and Wenger [15] constructed an infinite family of planar orgraphs \\(G\\) on \\(n\\) vertices with \\(fvs(G) = \\frac{n-1}{2}\\). ... Conjecture 3 (Neumann-Lara, 1985 [16]). ... Theorem 4 (Dreyer, Pinlou and Valicov, 2026 [19]).\n\nThe 2 obtained by Borodin [4] in 1979 directly imply that \\(fvs(G) \\le \\frac{3n}{5}\\) for planar oriented graphs \\(G\\) on \\(n\\) vertices. Surprisingly, this is still the best known upper bound for \\(fvs(G)\\) in planar oriented graphs.\n\nAlbertson posed the following weakening of 1 (see [13]–[15]): does every planar orgraph contain an acyclic set of size at least \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\)? In other words, is it true that \\(fvs(G) \\le \\frac{n}{2}\\) for any planar oriented graph \\(G\\) on \\(n\\) vertices? If true, the conjecture would be tight since Knauer, Valicov and Wenger [15] constructed an infinite family of planar orgraphs \\(G\\) on \\(n\\) vertices with \\(fvs(G) = \\frac{n-1}{2}\\). Moreover, the question is a weakening of the following famous conjecture.\n\nConjecture 3 (Neumann-Lara, 1985 [16]). Every planar oriented graph can be vertex-partitioned into two acyclic sets.\n\nWhile 3 remains open in general, it was established by Li and Mohar [17] for planar orgraphs of digirth at least 4. Therefore, \\(fvs(G) \\le \\frac{n}{2}\\) for planar oriented graphs \\(G\\) of digirth at least 4. As in the undirected case, better upper bounds for \\(fvs(G)\\) are known when the digirth \\(g\\) is large. For \\(g \\ge 6\\), Esperet, Lemoine and Maffray [18] proved that \\(fvs(G) \\le \\frac{2n-6}{g}\\). This result was recently improved by the author together with Pinlou and Valicov [19], who showed that \\(fvs(G) \\le \\frac{n-2}{g-2}\\) for planar orgraphs of digirth \\(g \\ge 3\\) and that this inequality is strict when \\(G\\) is not a directed cycle. They also showed the following inequality that will be used in this article to prove one of the corollaries.\n\nTheorem 4 (Dreyer, Pinlou and Valicov, 2026 [19]). Let \\(G\\) be a strongly connected planar oriented graph of digirth \\(g \\ge 4\\) having \\(n\\) vertices and \\(m\\) arcs. Then \\(fvs(G) \\le \\frac{n-2}{g-3} - \\frac{m}{g(g-3)}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the list version of a conjecture of Erdős and Neumann-Lara (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.01020)\nciteturn6academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Title: On the list version of a conjecture of Erdős and Neumann-Lara ... Date: Sun Mar  1 09:57:16 2026\nTitle: On the list version of a conjecture of Erdős and Neumann-Lara\nAuthors: Ararat Harutyunyan, Lucas Picasarri-Arrieta, Gil Puig i Surroca\nDate: Sun Mar  1 09:57:16 2026\n\nThe dichromatic number of a digraph $D$, denoted by $\\vecχ(D)$, is the smallest number of colours required to colour the vertices of $D$ such that each colour class induces an acyclic digraph. A conjecture of Erdős and Neumann-Lara states that there exists a function $f(k)$ such that for every graph $G$ with $χ(G) \\geq f(k)$ there is an orientation of $G$ such that the resulting digraph $D$ satisfies $\\vecχ(D) \\geq k$. We prove the list version of this conjecture: if $G$ has large list chromatic number then there is an orientation of $G$ such that the resulting digraph has large list dichromatic number. The main tool in our result is the following theorem, which is an extension of an analogous result of Alon for the chromatic number: every graph of minimum degree $d$ admits an orientation such that the resulting digraph has list dichromatic number of order at least $\\ln d$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuelques sujets sur la coloration des graphes orientés — Gil Puig i surroca · (2026) | TGRS Research Map | TGRS (https://globalresearchspace.com/space?star=W7143266243)\nciteturn6search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last m